"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Favorable findings include a negative surgical margin (0.0), no seminal vesicle invasion (0.0), and absence of lymphovascular invasion (0.0). The preoperative PSA was elevated at 5.7. The surgical pathology showed a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and pathological T2 stage with no extraprostatic extension. Countervailing factors include the absence of lymph node removal, resulting in a pNx status, which widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. The biopsy grade (Gleason 3+4) matched the prostatectomy specimen, which is a positive finding. The primary uncertainty lies in the missing postoperative PSA and subsequent trajectory, as this information is crucial for assessing recurrence risk. Missing nodal sampling also contributes to this uncertainty. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent changes would significantly revise the prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 45.23 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 29.40–61.06 months; its coverage has not been validated."
